---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import g from "../data/general-hewitt.json";

const cats = ["Married couple and children", "Single men", "Single women"];
---
<Base
  title="The General Hewitt"
  eyebrow={`${g.port}, ${g.arrived} · QSA ${g.item}, image ${g.image}`}
  dek="The archive knew this family reached Queensland. It did not know how, when, or on what. The passenger register gives all three — and a fourteen-year-old girl shipped as a domestic servant.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Eight of them sailed. On <strong>16 December 1854</strong> the
    <strong>General Hewitt</strong> put into Brisbane with <strong>394 souls</strong> aboard, and
    among them were <a href={u("/people/james-wakefield/")}>James Wakefield</a>, his wife Hannah,
    and six of their children — including <a href={u("/people/miriam-wakefield/")}>Miriam</a>, then
    fourteen.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>This was found the long way round.</strong> Queensland State Archives' own search
      refused to run — every query returned a system error. But Queensland publishes the
      <a href={g.indexUrl}>assisted-immigration index as open data ↗</a>, a plain CSV, and the whole
      family is in it. The index then names a digital image, <span class="mono">{g.image}</span>, and
      that turns out to be <strong>the register itself</strong>.
      <a href={g.imageUrl}>The scan, from QSA ↗</a>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <Blk label="Page 78, 80 and 81 of the register" title="The family, split across three categories">

    <p>An assisted-passage register does not list a family together. It lists
    <em>married couples with their young children</em>, then <em>single men</em>, then
    <em>single women</em> — so the Wakefields appear three pages apart, and the two eldest boys and
    the eldest girl aboard are entered as though they were travelling alone.</p>

    {cats.map((cat) => (
      <>
        <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.4rem">{cat} · page {g.rows.find((r) => r.cat === cat).pg}</div>
        <Scroll>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Country</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {g.rows.filter((r) => r.cat === cat).map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>{r.name}</strong>{r.note && <div class="small">{r.note}</div>}</td>
                  <td class="m">{r.age}</td>
                  <td class="small">{r.occ || "—"}</td>
                  <td>{r.county === "— ditto —"
                        ? <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{r.county}</span>
                        : <strong>{r.county}</strong>}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </Scroll>
      </>
    ))}
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Three things it confirms" title="A trade, a city, and a girl of fourteen">

    <ul class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>James Wakefield is entered as a MASON.</strong> The
      1841 census at <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>Drivers Fields</a> calls him a
      <em>Mason Journeyman</em>. Thirteen years and half a world apart, two clerks who never met wrote
      down the same trade. <span class="chip doc">documented twice</span></li>

      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>The three eldest children are from Bristol.</strong>
      Hiram, Aaron and Marion each get their own line and their own county, and all three say
      <strong>Bristol</strong> — exactly where the census and the birth registration put them.</li>

      <li><strong>Miriam is “Marion”, and she is a domestic servant.</strong> Fourteen years old,
      entered among the <em>single women</em> rather than with her parents, with an occupation
      against her name. Five years later she married
      <a href={u("/people/william-hartley-sneyd/")}>William Hartley Sneyd</a> at Brisbane.
      <a href={u("/the-women/")}>The women →</a></li>
    </ul>

    <Note>
      <strong>And one thing it pointedly does not give.</strong> Hannah's county is a
      <em>ditto mark</em> under her husband's <strong>Gloucester</strong> — the household head's
      county, entered once and carried down the page. It is not a statement about where she was born,
      and the register proves it knows the difference: on the very same day it enters her three
      eldest children as <strong>Bristol</strong>. <span class="chip inf">not her birthplace</span>
      <br /><br />
      So the question the last two days have been chasing is still open. The 1841 census says Hannah
      was born outside Gloucestershire — WHICH WAS WRONG, and the 1851 census names her birthplace as
      BERKELEY; the <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>reconstitution of all 81
      Gloucestershire Saniger households</a> says she is in none of them; and the one register that
      might have named her county dittoed it instead.
      <a href={u("/chew-magna/")}>Where to look now →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="The voyage" title="Who else was aboard">
    <p class="dek">The register closes with a summary in the clerk's hand.</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          {g.summary.map(([k, v]) => (
            <tr><td>{k}</td><td class="m"><strong>{v}</strong></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Reckoned for victualling as 325 “adults” — children
    counted as halves, infants not counted at all. The counties on these pages are Cambridge,
    Middlesex, Somerset, Essex, Kilkenny, Tipperary, Wicklow, Armagh, Cork, Roscommon and Dublin: an
    English and Irish cargo of farm labourers, masons, ploughmen, bricklayers, dairymaids and
    domestic servants, landed at a settlement that would not become a state capital for another five
    years.</p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What this changes</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The emigration is dated.</strong> The archive has said
      for years that the family "reached Queensland". It reached it on
      <strong>16 December 1854</strong>, aboard a named ship, with the ages of every passenger
      recorded. <span class="chip doc">documented</span></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>James Wakefield's trade is confirmed independently</strong>,
      and the family's Bristol origin with it.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Three daughters are missing from the manifest — and at
      least one of them was already dead.</strong> This page framed Eliza, Ellen and Martha as an open
      question about emigration. <strong>Eliza was buried at Bristol on 4 May 1834, aged nineteen
      months, and Ellen at St Paul, Portland Square on 12 July 1840, aged five</strong> — both
      baptised there to James and Hannah. And Martha, searched under every spelling and every record
      type, has no birth, baptism or burial at Bristol at all. <a href={u("/berkeley-woman/")}>The records →</a>
      <span class="chip doc">corrected 13 September 2026</span></li>
      <li><strong>Hannah's birthplace is not here</strong> — but it now has a candidate. A Saniger
      household two streets from the Wakefields' in 1851 gives its birthplace as Chew Magna in
      SOMERSET, which is the one thing the 1841 census insists on about her.
      <a href={u("/chew-magna/")}>Chew Magna →</a></li>
    </ol>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>The household they left, 1841 →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>The Sanigers they did not come from →</a> ·
      <a href={g.imageUrl}>The register scan, QSA ↗</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
